## Research summary

**Resolved artifact & deps.** `spring-aspects:6.0.0`'s POM pulls the identical `org.aspectj:aspectjweaver:1.9.9.1` that `5.3.31` already uses (verified against Maven Central) — the same version visible in the issue's own build report (`aspectjweaver-1.9.9.1.jar`). `spring-context`/`spring-tx`/`spring-beans`/`spring-core` at `6.0.0` show no new mandatory transitive deps relevant to this module. spring-aspects ships pre-woven (compile-time AspectJ-woven) classes, so no javaagent/load-time-weaving setup is involved.

**How the existing test uses the library.** The current `Spring_aspectsTest.java` exercises `@Configurable`, `AnnotationBeanConfigurerAspect`, and `AdviceMode.ASPECTJ` for caching/tx/async — explicitly `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)`, i.e. deliberately avoiding CGLIB proxying. This matters because sibling modules `spring-context` and `spring-aop` (already tested at `6.0.0` in this repo) *do* need extra native-image setup — a `spring.objenesis.ignore=true` system property plus a pre-generated-CGLIB-proxy-classes build step — specifically because their tests instantiate CGLIB proxies via Objenesis. `spring-tx` at `6.0.0`, which (like spring-aspects) doesn't exercise CGLIB proxy creation, needed no such setup — only its own extra `jakarta.transaction-api`/`javax.transaction-api` test deps for its own coverage, unrelated to spring-aspects.

**No Docker/DB/network involved** — build.gradle has no testcontainers or external service usage, and the module is a pure in-JVM AOP/DI test.

**Unsupportable-verdict check:** the issue's log is truncated right after a passing test (`genericInterfaceDrivenAspectConfiguresConfigurableObjects() SUCCESSFUL`); the actual failing operation is never shown. Per the rules, missing FAILURE evidence means the process stops there — repairable is the only justified conclusion, not skip_unsupported.

